    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A privacy fence is one of the bigger exterior projects a homeowner can take on. Done well, a garden fence gives you security, a tidier frontage and a private outdoor space for the family. Done badly, it can mean paying for the same fence twice, or more, once mistakes in planning and installation come to light.</p>
<p>From disputes over the boundary line to posts that lean after the first winter storm, the cost of putting things right usually lands on the homeowner. Understanding the common mistakes, and how professionals avoid them, is the best way to keep the project on budget and end up with a fence that stays straight and sound.</p>

<h2 id="property-lines">1. Getting the boundary wrong</h2>
<p>One of the most expensive mistakes is building a fence on your neighbour's land. Even a small encroachment can lead to a dispute and a demand to move it, and then you lose the materials, the labour of the first installation and the cost of taking it down. Many homeowners go by old plans or landmarks such as trees, hedges or an old fence, but these are often not where the legal boundary actually runs.</p>
<p>Before the first hole is dug, establish where your boundary is:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>England and Wales:</strong> check your title plan from HM Land Registry, but remember these plans generally show only general boundaries, not the exact line. Look at your deeds for any fencing obligations, talk to your neighbour before you start, and consider putting an agreed boundary in writing, rather like a party wall agreement. If there is any disagreement, a chartered surveyor who specialises in boundaries can help before it becomes a legal dispute.</li>
<li><strong>United States:</strong> a professional property survey will locate and mark your property pins, which is far cheaper than defending or rebuilding a fence later.</li>
</ul>
<p>Once you know the line, set the fence a little inside your boundary rather than exactly on it. That allows for posts that lean slightly over time and concrete footings that spread underground, so the whole fence stays on your land.</p>

<h2 id="local-codes">2. Ignoring planning rules and local codes</h2>
<p>Fence height and position are regulated almost everywhere, and the rules vary, so always check with your local planning authority or building department before you buy materials.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>England:</strong> a fence is generally permitted development, so it does not need planning permission, if it is no more than 2 m high, or no more than 1 m high next to a highway used by vehicles. That lower limit often catches out front boundaries. There are exceptions: listed buildings, conservation areas, areas covered by an Article 4 direction and properties with planning conditions restricting fences may need permission, so confirm with your local planning authority.</li>
<li><strong>United States:</strong> zoning, permit and HOA rules vary by town and neighbourhood. Many have sight-line rules that limit fence height near driveways and street corners so drivers can see pedestrians and traffic, and many HOAs specify which side of the fence faces the street (usually the finished side) and even the colour of the stain.</li>
</ul>
<p>Building without the right approval can mean enforcement action, fines or an order to lower or remove the fence. Check the rules first, get any approval in writing and keep the paperwork with your house documents.</p>

<h2 id="underground-utilities">3. Digging without checking for buried services</h2>
<p>Every post hole is a chance to hit a water pipe, a cable or, most dangerously, a gas pipe. Damaging a service can be hazardous, and you may be liable for the repair. Check before you dig:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>United States:</strong> call 811 a few days before digging so the utility companies can mark their lines.</li>
<li><strong>UK:</strong> ask the utility companies for plans of their apparatus near your property and scan the fence line with a cable avoidance tool (hire shops and fencing contractors often have one). Dig carefully by hand near any marked route.</li>
</ul>
<p>Also check your deeds for easements (US) or wayleaves and rights of access (UK). A utility company or a neighbour may have the right to reach pipes, cables or a shared path, and a fence across that route may have to come down, often at your expense. Plan the layout so access points stay clear.</p>

<h2 id="material-choice">4. Choosing the cheapest material</h2>
<p>The cheapest material is not always the cheapest fence. Pressure-treated softwood is budget-friendly, but it can warp, twist and shrink as it dries, and in a damp climate it tends to need replacing sooner than more durable options, especially if it is not maintained.</p>
<p>Western red cedar and good-quality vinyl (PVC) cost more upfront but usually last noticeably longer. Cedar contains natural oils that help it resist rot and insects, and vinyl needs very little maintenance. How long any fence lasts depends on the material, your climate, the installation and how well it is looked after, so compare the manufacturer's or supplier's warranty and think about the cost over the fence's life rather than the price on day one.</p>
<p>Wind matters too. A solid close-board fence acts like a sail and catches more wind than a slatted one. In exposed spots, consider a slatted or hit-and-miss design, which still gives good privacy but lets some wind through and puts less strain on the posts.</p>

<h2 id="post-depth">5. Setting posts too shallow</h2>
<p>A fence is only as strong as its posts. A common DIY mistake is not digging the holes deep enough. In cold climates, posts that are not set deep enough can be pushed up by the freeze-thaw cycle, a process known as frost heave.</p>
<p>As a common rule of thumb, posts go about a third to a quarter of their length below ground, deeper on exposed sites or in frost-prone soil. For a 1.8 m (6 ft) fence that often means around 60 cm (2 ft) in the ground, but follow the fence supplier's guidance for your post size, fence height and soil. Skimping on depth or concrete leads to a fence that leans and can eventually blow over, and fixing a leaning fence usually means pulling the posts and starting again.</p>

<h2 id="drainage">6. Ignoring drainage and ground clearance</h2>
<p>Water is the biggest enemy of a timber fence. If water pools around the posts and boards, constant moisture leads to rot, even in treated timber. A solid fence can also act like a dam on a sloping garden, holding water on one side and causing erosion or soggy ground.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Keep boards off the soil.</strong> In the UK, gravel boards (concrete or treated timber boards along the bottom of the fence) are the usual answer, because they take the wet and can be replaced on their own. Otherwise leave a small gap between the bottom of the boards and the ground.</li>
<li><strong>Choose stepped or racked on slopes.</strong> A stepped fence keeps each panel level and drops in steps down the slope; a racked fence follows the slope. Stepped suits panels, while racked suits fences built on site, but either way keep a clear gap at the bottom.</li>
<li><strong>Move the water away.</strong> Grading the soil away from the fence line or adding a gravel trench or French drain can stop water collecting at the posts.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="gate-hardware">7. Using lightweight gate hardware</h2>
<p>Gates are the only moving parts of a fence and usually the first to fail. Lightweight hinges on a heavy privacy gate soon let it sag, so the latch no longer lines up and the gate scrapes the ground. Fixing it can mean rebuilding the gate frame or replacing the gate post.</p>
<p>Use heavy-duty hinges and latches that are galvanised or powder-coated so they resist rust, and hang the gate on a strong, well-set post. Build the gate with an anti-sag diagonal brace running from the bottom hinge side up to the top latch side, so the frame stays square. For wide gates, a gate wheel or an anti-sag kit can take some of the weight.</p>

<h2 id="professional-labour">8. Hiring the wrong installer</h2>
<p>Fence building is hard, precise work. Professional installers have power augers, laser levels and experience of different soils, which a homeowner usually does not. A common mistake is accepting a suspiciously low quote, which can mean fewer bags of concrete, skipped permissions or lower-grade timber.</p>
<p>Vet any fencing contractor before you commit:</p>
<ul>
<li>Check they have public liability insurance.</li>
<li>Ask for references or recent jobs you can look at.</li>
<li>Get a written, itemised quote covering posts, panels or boards, gravel boards, gates, concrete, removal of the old fence and waste.</li>
<li>Ask what guarantee they give on their workmanship, and what the materials warranty covers.</li>
</ul>

<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Before you order anything, walk the fence line with your neighbour, your boundary documents and a list of any planning limits. Agreeing the line and the height up front prevents most of the costly problems on this list.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>How far should a fence be set back from the boundary?</h3>
<p>There is no single rule. Many homeowners set the fence a little inside their boundary so the posts, footings and any future lean stay on their own land. Check your deeds and any local rules, and agree the position with your neighbour.</p>
<h3>Is it better to nail or screw fence boards?</h3>
<p>Screws are generally better. Timber expands and contracts with the weather, which can work nails loose over time, while good exterior-grade screws grip more firmly and make it easier to replace a damaged board. Use fixings suitable for treated timber, as recommended by the timber supplier.</p>
<h3>Do I need to seal a new wood fence straight away?</h3>
<p>Follow the timber supplier's advice. Treated timber often needs time to dry before it will take a stain or sealer, because moisture in the wood stops the finish soaking in. Cedar can usually be finished sooner if you want to keep its colour. Read the finish manufacturer's instructions too.</p>
<h3>How long does a privacy fence last?</h3>
<p>It depends on the material, your climate, the quality of installation and how well it is maintained. As a rough comparison, cedar and good-quality vinyl usually outlast pressure-treated softwood. Check the manufacturer's or installer's warranty for its stated term, and keep up with cleaning and re-staining to get the most from a timber fence.</p>
<h3>Can I build a fence over an easement or wayleave?</h3>
<p>Sometimes, but it is risky. If a utility company or someone with a right of access needs to reach that land, they may be entitled to remove the fence and may not have to replace it. Check your deeds, title documents or property survey before you plan the layout.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>Most fence problems start before the first post goes in: the wrong boundary, the wrong height for local rules, buried services, shallow posts or poor drainage. Get those right and choose materials and hardware that suit your garden, and the fence will stay straight and private. For the rest of the garden, see our <a href="/blog/landscape-edging-pros-and-cons">landscape edging pros and cons</a> guide and <a href="/blog/small-yard-wood-deck-ideas">small yard wood deck ideas</a>, or explore our <a href="/services/balcony-terrace-decoration">balcony and terrace decoration</a> service. To plan your outdoor space with a designer, <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a>.</p>
